      "speaker": "Aleksandra Olejak",
      "title": "Formation Pathways of Gravitational-Wave Sources: Isolated Binary Evolution vs. Dynamical Channels",
      "abstract": "The rapidly expanding database of gravitational-wave (GW) detections, with new results from the O4 run, offers unprecedented insights into the origins of compact object mergers. Black hole–black hole systems encode in their masses and spin distributions the signatures of their evolutionary histories. Interactions in massive star binaries, through stable or unstable mass transfer, are a key aspect of isolated binary evolution and are expected to accompany the formation of many GW sources. Such interactions can leave characteristic imprints that distinguish isolated binary evolution from dynamical formation channels, where compact objects assemble through stellar encounters in dense environments. Identifying these fingerprints in the observed population is therefore crucial for disentangling the relative contributions of different channels.  In my talk, I will discuss different formation channels and their possible observational signatures, comparing predictions from isolated binary evolution to dynamical channels. I will highlight key fingerprints, such as the significant fraction of unequal-mass mergers, which may point to stable mass transfer and mass-ratio reversal in binaries. The observed spin distribution, dominated by relatively low BH spins but including a subset of highly spinning systems, suggests efficient angular momentum transport in massive stars, along with tidal spin-up of some second-born black holes. However, such a distribution appears to be in tension with the typically high spins inferred from X-ray binaries. These features provide valuable clues about both binary evolution and the physics of massive stars."
