      "speaker": "Alberto Colombo",
      "title": "Present and Future of Multi-Messenger Astronomy: Binary Neutron Star and Black Hole–Neutron Star Mergers",
      "abstract": " The landmark detection of GW170817 — a gravitational-wave signal from a binary neutron star merger — by the LIGO and Virgo collaborations, and its multi-wavelength electromagnetic counterparts, inaugurated the era of multi-messenger astronomy. Since then, a second binary neutron star merger, GW190425, has been observed, though without any associated electromagnetic emission. Similarly, black hole–neutron star mergers have been confirmed through gravitational-wave observations, yet no electromagnetic counterpart has been detected so far. During the third observing run (O3) of the global gravitational-wave detector network, and throughout the first two parts of the fourth run (O4a,b), extensive follow-up campaigns across the electromagnetic spectrum were carried out. Despite the significant observational effort, these searches have mostly revealed unrelated transients — particularly Type Ia supernovae — offering only limited insights into the progenitors of the gravitational-wave events.  As we look ahead to the remainder of O4, the upcoming O5 run, and the next generation of observatories such as the Einstein Telescope and Cosmic Explorer, the need for accurate forecasts becomes increasingly crucial. Reliable predictions of merger rates and their electromagnetic signatures will be key to optimizing follow-up strategies and enhancing our chances of capturing the rapidly fading signals associated with these peculiar events.  In this seminar, I will present realistic projections for the number and properties of binary neutron star and black hole–neutron star mergers expected to be observed as multi-messenger sources with current and future detectors. These results aim to guide the community toward more efficient and effective observational strategies in the coming decade."
