      "abstract": "Be stars are rapidly rotating early-type main sequence stars surrounded by a decretion disk responsible for the emission components in their Balmar lines. Be stars show long and short timescale complex light variations. Recent long-baseline precise photometries from space have revealed the presence of characteristic groups of oscillation frequencies in addition to some discrete frequencies. I will talk about that those oscillations to be caused by r modes (Rossby modes), and overstable convective (OsC) modes in the convective core coupled with g modes in the envelope (core rotates slightly faster than the envelope). Fitting theoretical oscillation frequencies to the observed ones, we can infer  the rotation rates in the convective core and in the envelope of each Be star."
