      "title": "Stellar atmosphere modelling as a fundamental tool to understand the properties and impact of hot, massive stars",
      "abstract": "Hot, massive stars are astrophysical keystones which shape their environment and enrich their host galaxies with matter. Most quantitative conclusions we draw about massive stars depend on their properties. However, measuring them is not an easy task and requires both excellent spectroscopy and well as sophisticated stellar atmosphere models. My Emmy Noether Group on stellar atmospheres and mass loss focusses on this interesting conjunction of observational, theoretical and numerical astrophysics. After introducing the basic techniques and challenges of atmosphere modelling for hot, massive stars and their inherent radiation-driven winds, I will present a selection of recent research efforts in my group and within a network of collaborators to illustrate the range of empirical and theoretical applications of modern non-LTE stellar atmosphere models and their results, such as the analysis of important landmarks of massive star evolution, the impact of stellar winds in clusters, or new theoretical insights on radiation-driven winds."
