      "type": "seminar",
      "date": "2026-01-08",
      "time": "10:00",
      "place": "Astrotech Jura",
      "speaker": "Loris Berthelot",
      "title": "Deep learning for filament detection",
      "abstract": "In galaxies, stars form within filaments made of gas and dust. These filaments arise in the interstellar medium and evolve according to the surrounding physical conditions and the star formation they host. They are observed with ground- and space-based instruments and studied through numerical simulations. Detecting these structures in data—both observational and simulated—is a necessary prerequisite for investigating the very earliest phases of star formation. Many algorithms have been developed to detect such filaments. They rely on parameters that describe the properties of the target filaments, in particular their contrast with respect to the surrounding emission. Results show that it is difficult to detect all filaments. In particular, low-contrast and/or low-density filaments are often missed, leading to a detection bias toward brighter filaments or toward fainter filaments that happen to lie in regions of low background emission. Moreover, these methods are sensitive to parameter settings that depend strongly on the data. With the rise of machine learning in many scientific and application fields, the use of neural networks has been proposed to overcome the limitations of filament detection algorithms and perform filament extraction through segmentation tasks. In this talk, I will present two complementary contributions. First, I will describe how we train semantic-segmentation networks on the Hi-GAL (Herschel) Galactic Plane maps to extract filaments from incomplete and heterogeneous labels, using a dedicated pipeline (tiling and semi-supervised learning) tailored to astrophysical data. I will also introduce PE-UNet, a U-Net variant that injects galactic position as auxiliary information and improves segmentation quality. Second, because assessing completeness and false detections is difficult on observations alone, I will present a synthetic benchmark dataset based on the Plummer radial profile, designed to evaluate both classical filament-finding algorithms and modern semantic/instance segmentation models, and to quantify the biases induced when training on biased observational extractions."
