      "abstract": "In this seminar I will present the fundamental role of mass transfer in shaping two of the most exciting and rapidly growing observed populations: stripped stars and gravitational wave sources. Next-generation gravitational wave detectors are expected to deliver hundreds of thousands of detections per year, and thousands of stripped stars are predicted to hide in the Milky Way, yet major uncertainties still persist in our understanding of mass transfer physics. This limits our ability to interpret the population of gravitational waves observed by the LIGO, Virgo and KAGRA interferometers, and the growing sample of post-interaction systems revealed by UV surveys. Using insights from detailed stellar structure models and observational constraints, I will outline the role of the interior physics of massive stars during mass transfer, and current open questions in our modeling frameworks."
