      "speaker": "Maud Gull",
      "title": "A Panchromatic Study of Metal-Poor Massive Stars in the Local Group",
      "abstract": "Massive metal-poor stars are central to a broad range of astrophysical phenomena, including cosmic reionization, chemical enrichment, and compact object formation. Here, I will present results from a joint HST UV/optical/IR photometric study and Keck and MMT optical spectroscopic study of massive stars in the nearest low-metallicity star-forming galaxy, Leo A (Z ~ 5% Z_sun; D ~ 800 kpc). This study derives stellar parameters for one of the largest samples of massive stars at sub-10% solar metallicity. I will show early indications that panchromatic UV–IR photometry can robustly constrain the properties of the massive main-sequence population. This approach offers a powerful complement to low-resolution spectroscopy, especially when designing observational strategies or trying to understand galaxy-wide stellar populations. I will also showcase a novel use of JWST and HST imaging data to identify and characterize metal-poor massive binaries, aiming to address uncertainties surrounding binary evolution. This includes the discovery of the most metal-poor massive contact binary candidate known to date in WLM (~14% Z_sun). I will present the lowest-metallicity spectroscopic sample (~5% Z_sun) of Be stars observed to date and comment on their putative companions and their fraction as a function of metallicity.  I will conclude by highlighting the opportunities that next-generation facilities will open for studying massive stars in the low-metallicity regime."
