      "date": "2026-06-19",
      "time": "10:00",
      "place": "Astrotech Jura",
      "speaker": "Sabina Manduca",
      "title": "Bridging Galactic Archaeology and binary compact object formation: Understanding the Cosmic History of R-Process Enrichment",
      "abstract": "The rapid neutron-capture process (r-process) is a key nucleosynthetic mechanism responsible for the production of the heaviest elements in the Universe and requires extremely neutron-rich environments. Compact-object mergers, including binary neutron star and neutron star–black hole systems, are among the most promising astrophysical sites for r-process nucleosynthesis. The chemical abundances observed in ancient stars of the Milky Way provide constraints on the cosmic history of r-process enrichment through Galactic archaeology. However, a discrepancy exists between the inferred enrichment history and the predictions of population synthesis models for merging binary neutron stars. Here we present the results of a study of the delay-time distribution  and the black-hole mass distribution of neutron star–black hole mergers, performed using the second version of the binary population synthesis code POSYDON (POpulation SYnthesis with Detailed binary-evolution simulatiONs)."
